# Building and running locally

Load the compiler once per shell: `. .\scripts\dev-env.ps1`. Sourcing it twice
is a no-op by design; re-running `VsDevCmd` yields a reduced environment whose
`PATH` has lost System32, so the script refuses unless given `-Force`.

## The camera worker is enabled by the local preset

`capture_worker_camera` only exists as a build target when the camera worker is
enabled during **configure**, and building without it fails with
`ninja: error: unknown target 'capture_worker_camera'`.

Copy `CMakeUserPresets.example.json` → `CMakeUserPresets.json` (gitignored).
That file carries both `CAPTURE_ENABLE_CAMERA_WORKER=ON` and
`GSTREAMER_1_0_ROOT_MSVC_X86_64`, so neither has to be passed by hand:

```powershell
cmake --preset windows-release-local
cmake --build build/windows-release
```

They live in the user presets rather than `CMakePresets.json` because configure
fails hard when the flag is on without GStreamer, which is the normal state for
CI and for anyone who does not touch the camera worker.

Never let the CMake Tools extension configure on its own: it does not pass the
flag and will quietly drop the target. `.vscode/settings.json` disables that.

## Stop the daemon before building

A running `capture_daemon` holds `capture_worker_camera.exe` open and the link
fails with `LNK1168`. `.cursor/hooks/` stops these automatically before a build;
stop them by hand if building outside the agent.

## Running the daemon

Use `tools/run-daemon-gstreamer.ps1`, or set `GSTREAMER_1_0_ROOT_MSVC_X86_64`,
prepend its `bin` to `PATH`, and set `CAPTURE_USE_CAMERA_WORKER=1` plus
`CAPTURE_CAMERA_WORKER_EXE`. Set `CAPTURE_WORKER_STDERR_LOG` to capture worker
stderr, which is otherwise invisible because workers are spawned with
`CREATE_NO_WINDOW`.

## Python interpreters differ on this workstation

`C:\Python314\python.exe` is first on `PATH`, has no pytest, and is outside the
`>=3.12,<3.13` range `pyproject.toml` requires. Use the 3.12 install:

```powershell
& "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\Programs\Python\Python312\python.exe" -m pytest tests/protocol -q
```

Do not set `PYTHONPATH` for this. `tool.pytest.ini_options.pythonpath` in
`pyproject.toml` already adds the two libraries plus `workers/python_host`,
`desktop`, and `tools`. The editor is the only thing that needs the paths
restated, via `python.analysis.extraPaths`.

`ruff` is not on the `PATH` that `dev-env.ps1` installs; it lives at
`$env:LOCALAPPDATA\Programs\Python\Python312\Scripts\ruff.exe`. CI runs
`ruff check` but not `ruff format`, so treat formatting as advisory.

Either interpreter works for the `tools/probe_*.py` scripts.
